  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "KENT L. JONES, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0000-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 4,
                "start": 4
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to KENT L. JONES, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner, the petitioner argues that the validity of the lien is the first question; if the lien is valid, there is no need to address the fraudulent conveyance issue."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "KENT L. JONES, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0000-0005"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 7,
                "start": 7
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to KENT L. JONES, ESQ., on behalf of the Petitioner, the petitioner asserts that the comprehensive text of the Federal tax lien reaches all property and all rights to property, and that the Court has consistently held that the lien reaches every valuable, legally protected right or interest of the taxpayer."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Kent L. Jones",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0003-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 56,
                "start": 56
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Kent L. Jones, the petitioner contends that the tenancy was destroyed when it was transferred from the husband to the spouse and again when the wife sold it to a third party."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "United States (Petitioner)",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0001-0005"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 28,
                "start": 28
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), according to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), the respondent argues that the 1975 Michigan statute granting spouses equal rights to rent and income in a tenancy by the entirety does not apply because the tenancy at issue was created in 1972, making the statute irrelevant."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0001-0006"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 29,
                "start": 29
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), according to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), the respondent points out that under Michigan law (Muskegon Lumber, 1953), a tenancy by the entirety continues even after the spouse receives proceeds, so the tenancy is not terminated by such transfers."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "2924a7156c2098f2cbecc5ccc380537b4eb993e4194a45c405e0577aaab0d6b0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-2924a7156c20-0001-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 28,
                "start": 28
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), according to Jeffrey S. Sutton (Respondent), the respondent notes that Michigan\u2019s 1975 statute states that spouses in a tenancy by the entirety have equal rights to rent, income, and profits, but emphasizes that this statute applies only to tenancies created after 1975."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Sandra L. Craft (Respondent)",
